@Module – class that provides dependencies.
@Provides – method inside @Module that returns the dependency instance.
@Inject – requests a dependency injection (used on constructor, field, or method).
@Component – bridge between @Module and @Inject.
@Singleton – ensures only one instance of the dependency is created.
@Scope – defines a custom lifecycle scope.
@Qualifier – allows multiple instances of the same type.
@Binds – binds an interface to its implementation in an abstract module.

@Test – marks a method as a test case.
@Before – runs before each test (setup).
@BeforeClass – runs once before all tests in the class.
@After – runs after each test (teardown).
@AfterClass – runs once after all tests.
@Ignore – skips the test.
@Test(timeout=500) – fails the test if it exceeds the specified time.

SupervisorScope is a coroutine builder that creates a new child scope and acts as a supervisor for coroutines launched within it.

Unlike a regular coroutine scope, if a child coroutine fails with an exception, the SupervisorScope does not cancel the remaining child coroutines. Each child is independent.

Example:
suspend fun myCoroutine() {
    supervisorScope {
        launch {
            // do some work
        }
        launch {
            // do some other work — runs even if the first launch fails
        }
    }
}

By default, all classes and functions in Kotlin are final, meaning they cannot be inherited or overridden. To allow inheritance or overriding, you must explicitly mark the class or function with the open keyword.

Example:
open class Animal {
    open fun speak() { }
}

class Dog : Animal() {
    override fun speak() { }
}

A higher-order function is a function that either takes another function as a parameter, returns a function, or both.

Example:
fun passMeFunction(abc: () -> Unit) {
    // executes the passed function
    abc()
}

Higher-order functions are the foundation for features like scope functions (let, apply, run), collection operations (map, filter), and coroutine builders (launch, async).

Primary Constructor:
Defined in the class header. It cannot contain any code directly; initialization logic goes in an init{} block.
Example: class Person(val name: String, val age: Int)

Secondary Constructor:
Defined inside the class body using the constructor keyword. You must explicitly call the primary constructor from a secondary constructor using this().

Example:
class Person(val name: String) {
    constructor(name: String, age: Int) : this(name) {
        // additional setup
    }
}

In Kotlin, a Singleton is created using the object keyword. The Kotlin compiler ensures only one instance is ever created.

Example:
object DatabaseManager {
    fun getConnection() { }
}

This is equivalent to Java's double-checked locking Singleton pattern but is much simpler and thread-safe by default.

Kotlin does not have a static keyword like Java. Instead, we use a companion object to define members that belong to the class itself rather than to any instance. A companion object allows you to call methods or access properties without creating an object of the class.

Example:
class Mentor {
    companion object {
        fun guide() {
            println("Guiding...")
        }
    }
}

// Call without creating an instance:
Mentor.guide()

This is the Kotlin equivalent of Java's static methods and static fields.

crossinline is used in inline functions to prevent non-local returns from a lambda parameter.

By default, a lambda passed to an inline function can use return to exit the outer calling function (non-local return). crossinline disallows this, ensuring the lambda can only return from itself.

Example:
inline fun execute(crossinline action: () -> Unit) {
    val runnable = Runnable { action() }
    runnable.run()
}

Use crossinline when the lambda will be executed in a different execution context, such as inside a Runnable or a callback, where a non-local return would be unsafe or ambiguous.

A Collector is the consumer of a Flow. It collects the items emitted by the Flow Builder after they have been transformed by operators.

Example:
viewModelScope.launch {
    myFlow
        .map { it * 2 }
        .collect { value ->
            println(value)
        }
}

The collect{} block is the most common terminal operator and acts as the collector. The flow does not start emitting until a collector begins collecting.

The flowOn operator controls which thread or dispatcher the upstream flow (the emission and transformation logic) runs on. It does not affect the collector, which runs on the scope's dispatcher.

Example:
val flow = flow {
    (0..10).forEach {
        delay(500)
        emit(it)
    }
}
.flowOn(Dispatchers.Default)

viewModelScope.launch {
    flow.collect { value ->
        // collected on Main thread (viewModelScope default)
        println(value)
    }
}

Here, the emission happens on Dispatchers.Default (background), but collection happens on the main thread.

The zip operator combines the emissions of two Flow collections together via a specified function and emits a single item for each combined pair.

Key advantage: Both flows run in parallel, and the result is delivered in a single callback only when both flows have emitted their corresponding values.

Example:
fun startLongRunningTask() {
    viewModelScope.launch {
        doLongRunningTaskOne()
            .zip(doLongRunningTaskTwo()) { resultOne, resultTwo ->
                return@zip resultOne + resultTwo
            }
            .flowOn(Dispatchers.Default)
            .catch { e ->
                // handle exception
            }
            .collect {
                // combined result
            }
    }
}

Use zip when you need to run two tasks in parallel and combine their results once both complete.

In Kotlin Flow, exceptions can be caught using the catch operator. It intercepts any exception thrown upstream (in the flow builder or operators) and lets you handle it gracefully without crashing.

Example:
(1..5).asFlow()
    .map {
        check(it != 3) { "Error at value $it" } // throws exception when value is 3
        it * it
    }
    .onCompletion {
        println("Flow completed")
    }
    .catch { e ->
        println("Caught exception: $e")
    }
    .collect {
        println(it)
    }

Note: The catch operator only catches exceptions from upstream. Exceptions thrown inside the collect block are not caught by catch and must be handled separately with a try-catch.

An init block is an initializer block that runs immediately after the primary constructor executes. Since you cannot write logic directly inside a primary constructor in Kotlin, the init block is the place to perform initialization operations.

A class can have one or more init blocks, and they execute in the order they appear in the class body.

Example:
class Person(val name: String) {
    init {
        println("Person created with name: $name")
    }
    init {
        println("Second init block runs after the first")
    }
}

val p = Person("Alice")
// Output:
// Person created with name: Alice
// Second init block runs after the first

@JvmStatic:
Tells the compiler to generate a true static method for a companion object function, making it accessible from Java code as a static method.
Example:
class Utils {
    companion object {
        @JvmStatic
        fun getAddress(): String = "MindOrks"
    }
}
// In Java: Utils.getAddress() — works as a static call

@JvmOverloads:
Generates overloaded versions of a Kotlin function that has default parameter values, so Java callers can call it without providing all arguments.
Example:
data class Event(val name: String, val date: Date = Date())
// Java can now call: new Event("MindOrks") without the date parameter

@JvmField:
Exposes a Kotlin property as a plain Java field, removing the auto-generated getter and setter. Used when Java code needs direct field access without calling get/set methods.

A SAM (Single Abstract Method) interface, also called a Functional Interface, is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method.

In Kotlin, it is declared using the fun interface keyword. SAM conversion allows you to pass a concise lambda expression instead of writing a verbose anonymous object.

Example:
fun interface OnClickAction {
    fun onClick()
}

// Without SAM (verbose anonymous object):
val action = object : OnClickAction {
    override fun onClick() {
        println("Clicked")
    }
}

// With SAM conversion (concise lambda):
val action = OnClickAction { println("Clicked") }

Kotlin also supports SAM conversion for Java interfaces like Runnable and OnClickListener, which is why you can write:
button.setOnClickListener { view -> println("Button clicked") }
instead of creating an anonymous OnClickListener object.

A Lambda in Kotlin is an anonymous function that can be stored in a variable, passed as a parameter, or returned from another function. It is mainly used to write concise, reusable code without creating separate function declarations.

Lambdas are commonly used with higher-order functions, collection operations like map and filter, and click listeners.

Lambda stored in a variable:
val square: (Int) -> Int = { value -> value * value }
println(square(3)) // 9

val addition: (Int, Int) -> Int = { num1, num2 -> num1 + num2 }
println(addition(4, 6)) // 10

Lambda passed as a parameter:
fun passFunction(action: () -> Unit) {
    action()
}
passFunction { println("Lambda executed") }

Lambda returned from a function:
fun returnAddFunction(): (Int, Int) -> Int {
    return { a, b -> a + b }
}
val add = returnAddFunction()
println(add(10, 20)) // 30

Real-world Android example using lambdas as callbacks in a ViewModel:
class RecipeViewModel : ViewModel() {
    fun fetchRecipes(
        onSuccess: (List<Recipe>) -> Unit,
        onError: (String) -> Unit
    ) {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            try {
                val recipes = repository.getRecipes()
                onSuccess(recipes)
            } catch (e: Exception) {
                onError(e.message ?: "Unknown Error")
            }
        }
    }
}

// Calling the function with lambda arguments:
viewModel.fetchRecipes(
    onSuccess = { recipes -> adapter.submitList(recipes) },
    onError = { message -> showToast(message) }
)
